# A program that keeps committing memory is stopped by the harness memory cap
# and reported as '[memory limit]': the .NET 10 JIT was seen allocating 40+ GB
# on bad IL. The cap is a Job Object on Windows and RLIMIT_DATA inside Linux,
# where the WSL runner runs msil-run.py itself (plan section 12, M0 row);
# there the runtime's out-of-memory message is what msil-run.py recognizes as
# '[memory limit]'. The program commits up to 4 GiB in 1 MiB steps and touches
# none of it, and it imports nothing, so the transcript is the same on every OS
# and runner.

# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t.dll %S/Inputs/memory-hog.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} within 60 %msil_exec --mem-limit-mb 256 --expect %S/Inputs/memory-hog --out %t.out %t.dll
# RUN: %{harness_check} equals %t.out "[memory limit]"
